preConditionHook:
// THE BAR REACHES THE RECENT EXTREME. Long: the high trades up to the highest
// high of the `lookback` bars ENDING ONE BAR AGO, within toleranceTicks. Short:
// the low trades down to the lowest low of that same window. A recent swing
// extreme is the magnet the market most often comes back to test, and the level
// a double top or double bottom forms at.
//
// THE WINDOW EXCLUDES THE CURRENT BAR, which is what separates this from
// HighestHighBreakout, whose window INCLUDES bar 0 and therefore means 'this bar
// made the N-bar high'. That case is this rule's boundary at toleranceTicks 0.
// The one-bar-shifted PRICE SERIES is handed to Highest/Lowest in the twin, as
// StopBeyondRecentRangeExtreme and NearAnyLevel write it, rather than lagging
// the function's own result.
//
// Highest/Lowest bottom out in Extremes(), which is EL SOURCE: seeded at the
// window's first bar, scanning the rest, strict comparison so a tie keeps the
// earliest bar, and its comparisons carry the relational tolerance -- hence
// el_gt / el_lt rather than > and <.
//
// THE TOLERANCE IS THE WHOLE POINT: Brooks counts the bars that REACH the level,
// including the ones that stop a tick short of taking it out, so the test is
// ONE-SIDED with a tick margin and never a two-sided band on the close. That is
// the touch convention every level rule in this slice follows. toleranceTicks 0
// is the strict 'the market has to get that extra tick and actually touch it'
// reading; 1 is the merged default.
//
// Tick division-first, toleranceTicks * (ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale()),
// computed once per bar into a local since both are per-symbol constants.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: lookback.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
levelHigh = high[1];
levelLow = low[1];
for (ii = 2; ii <= (int)lookback; ++ii) {
    if (el_gt(high[ii], levelHigh)) levelHigh = high[ii];
    if (el_lt(low[ii], levelLow)) levelLow = low[ii];
}
